Subject: Mark `const`-qualified types with a newtype wrapper

Rust cannot spell a top-level `const`, so bindgen drops it: `const int` and
`int` both come out `c_int`, and `int *const` and `int *` both come out
`*mut c_int`. Constness of a *pointee* survives, because `*const T` says it,
but the qualifier on the type itself does not survive anywhere.

That loses facts consumers need. A `const` data member can delete the class's
implicitly declared default constructor - it does whenever the member has no
default member initializer and is not itself const-default-constructible, which
is every scalar member - and a generator which cannot see the qualifier will
offer a constructor C++ refuses to compile. A `const`-qualified return type is
part of the function's type, so anything which takes the address of the
function has to name it exactly.

`--use-const-newtype-wrapper` wraps such a type in `__bindgen_marker_Const<T>`,
the same shape `--use-opaque-newtype-wrapper` and
`--use-reference-newtype-wrapper` already use: a fake type the caller declares
itself, through `--raw-line` or by post-processing. Off by default, so no
existing output changes.

The wrap is at the tail of `TryToRustTy for Type`, so the positions a type
reaches from there are covered without enumerating any of them. Two of those
also carry a value - a `const` variable's type sits beside the initializer, and
a `const` bitfield's accessors cast between the field type and the allocation
unit's integer - so the definition the caller supplies has to be transparent
(`pub type __bindgen_marker_Const<T> = T;`) rather than a newtype struct. The
option's documentation says so. The one place which has to *name* the type
rather than declare something of it, a newtype alias's constructor expression,
unwraps the marker itself.

One known gap, where an existing resolution step erases the qualifier before
the wrap site sees it, and not addressed here: an alias's target is resolved
`through_type_refs()` before `pub type`/`pub use` is emitted for it, so
`typedef const E CE` over an enum loses it. A template argument had the same
problem when the argument was a record; `19-const-template-argument.patch`
fixes that one, the same way the pointer arm here already does.

